STAGE 1: INSTRUCTION FETCH (IF)
🧱 Brick 1: The Program Counter Register (pc_reg.v)
 * What it is: A 32-bit hardware register containing 32 microscopic memory cells (D Flip-Flops).
 * What it does: It holds the address of the instruction we are currently reading. It features a built-in adder that calculates PC + 4 on every clock beat (each instruction takes up 4 bytes of space). If a branch or jump evaluates to true, it overrides the +4 and leaps to the target address instead.
🧱 Brick 2: Instruction Memory (imem.v)
 * What it is: The library where the program's compiled instructions live.
 * What it does: It receives the memory address from the Program Counter, looks inside its storage table, and outputs the raw 32-bit binary instruction word waiting at that slot.
 * Special Trick: Memory is organized in words (groups of 4 bytes). We drop the lowest two address bits (addr[31:2]) so we index cleanly without skipping lines.
BARRIER 1: THE FIRST AIRLOCK
🧱 Brick 3: The IF/ID Pipeline Register (if_id_reg.v)
 * What it is: A physical wall of flip-flops placed between Stage 1 and Stage 2.
 * What it does: It captures the fetched instruction on the clock tick and holds it completely steady so Stage 2 can study it, while Stage 1 is already moving forward to fetch the next instruction.
 * Safety Valve (Flush): If a branch jumps in Stage 3, this register has a flush wire that instantly wipes its stored instruction to zero (NOP or "Do Nothing") so the wrong speculative instruction is discarded.
STAGE 2: INSTRUCTION DECODE & OPERAND FETCH (ID)
🧱 Brick 4: The Control Unit (control_unit.v)
 * What it is: The central traffic controller of the processor.
 * What it does: It reads the opcode (the first 7 bits of the instruction) and asserts or clears control signals:
   * RegWrite: "Get ready to save an answer into a register."
   * ALUSrc: "Hey ALU, do math with another register or with a hardcoded constant number?"
   * MemRead / MemWrite: "Turn Data RAM on or off."
   * is_cheat: "This is our custom opcode—turn on the PopCount accelerator!"
   * is_alu_op: "This is standard math—tell the Eco-Gate to wake up the ALU."
🧱 Brick 5: The Register File (regfile.v)
 * What it is: The fast-access scratchpad of the CPU. It contains 32 registers (x0 through x31), each 32 bits wide.
 * What it does:
   * It can read two source values (rs1 and rs2) simultaneously at any moment without waiting for a clock tick.
   * It writes results back to the destination register (rd) strictly when the clock ticks.
   * The Holy Rule of RISC-V: Register x0 is physically soldered to Ground (0 Volts). It always evaluates to 0x00000000. Any calculation attempting to overwrite x0 is safely discarded by hardware.
🧱 Brick 6: The Immediate Generator (imm_gen.v)
 * What it is: An un-scrambler and sign-extension circuit.
 * What it does: Often, an instruction includes a hardcoded number inside itself (like the 5 in addi x1, x0, 5). RISC-V stores these numbers across different sliced bit positions depending on the instruction format (I, S, B, U, J). This module extracts the bits, puts them back in numeric order, and extends them to a full 32-bit signed number.
BARRIER 2: THE SECOND AIRLOCK
🧱 Brick 7: The ID/EX Pipeline Register (id_ex_reg.v)
 * What it is: A very wide barrier register (over 150 flip-flops side-by-side).
 * What it does: It takes all the decoded control wires, register data values, register addresses, and immediate numbers from Stage 2 and holds them static for Stage 3. It also features a flush pin to erase incorrect instructions when a branch misprediction occurs.
STAGE 3: EXECUTE, MEMORY & WRITEBACK (EX / MEM / WB)
🧱 Brick 8: The Eco-Gate Unit (eco_gate.v)
 * What it is: An array of 2-to-1 multiplexers (electronic switches) guarding the ALU inputs.
 * What it does: It monitors the is_alu_op wire. If the instruction is a memory access (lw, sw) or a branch comparison (beq), it forces the ALU inputs to flat 0s. The internal adder transistors remain still, preventing wasted dynamic switching power.
🧱 Brick 9: The Master ALU + Cheat-Code Accelerator (alu_top.v)
 * What it is: The mathematical engine of the core.
 * What it does:
   * Runs standard arithmetic: add, sub, bitwise and, or, xor, shifts (sll, srl, sra), and comparisons (slt, sltu).
   * Houses our custom PopCount Engine: an optimized hardware adder-tree that counts all set binary 1 bits in a register within one clock cycle.
   * Outputs a zero flag: tells the branch unit whether two compared numbers were equal.
🧱 Brick 10: The Branch Resolution Unit (branch_unit.v)
 * What it is: The decision-making unit for loops and if/else checks.
 * What it does: It checks whether a condition was met (e.g., Branch if Equal). If true:
   * It asserts branch_taken = 1.
   * It calculates the destination address: \text{Target} = \text{PC} + \text{Immediate}.
   * It overrides the Program Counter in Stage 1 and flushes the pipeline barriers.
🧱 Brick 11: Data Memory (dmem.v)
 * What it is: The RAM workspace of the processor where program variables, arrays, and memory buffers live.
 * What it does:
   * If executing a Store (sw), it saves a 32-bit register value into a memory slot.
   * If executing a Load (lw), it retrieves a 32-bit value from a memory slot so it can be committed back into a register.
🧱 Brick 12: The Forwarding Unit (forwarding_unit.v)
 * What it is: An intelligent bypass circuit that eliminates execution stalls.
 * What it does: Suppose instruction 1 calculates x1 = 5 + 2, and instruction 2 immediately needs to compute x3 = x1 + 10.
   Without forwarding, instruction 2 would have to wait for instruction 1 to write its result back into the Register File.
   The Forwarding Unit detects this hazard, grabs the calculated answer directly off the ALU output wire, and feeds it straight back into the ALU input for the next cycle!
   * Critical Safety Guard: It strictly verifies ex_rd != 0, ensuring it never accidentally forwards non-zero values intended for register x0.
